    <string name="drawer_contact_email">tomasz.pieczara@gazeta.pl</string>
    <string name="drawer_privacy_body">Privacy policy — LabDroid %1$s\n\nLabDroid reads data from sensors, location, camera and other hardware components exclusively on your device, only to display it inside the app or to let you export it yourself. No data ever leaves your device automatically: there are no remote servers, no analytics SDKs, no ad networks and no third parties involved.\n\nThe only data that leaves your device is a file you explicitly choose to export or share using your own device\'s share/export function.\n\nBecause no personal data is collected, stored remotely or processed by us, there is no profiling and no cross-app tracking. Since all processing happens locally on your device, you remain fully in control of your data at all times, and uninstalling the app removes everything it has ever produced.\n\nContact for any privacy-related question: tomasz.pieczara@gazeta.pl.</string>
    <string name="drawer_compatibility_body">LabDroid relies exclusively on official, public Android APIs to read information about sensors, hardware, location, camera and connectivity. All permissions are declared in the app manifest and requested at runtime following Google\'s guidelines.\n\nSupported Android versions: Android 8.0 Oreo (API 26) and newer, up to and including the latest Android 16 (API 36):\n\n• Android 8.0 / 8.1 Oreo (API 26-27)\n• Android 9 Pie (API 28)\n• Android 10 (API 29)\n• Android 11 (API 30)\n• Android 12 / 12L (API 31-32)\n• Android 13 (API 33)\n• Android 14 (API 34)\n• Android 15 (API 35)\n• Android 16 (API 36)\n\nSome hardware-dependent readings (e.g. flash strength levels, battery cycle count) are only available on newer Android versions or specific devices; the app clearly marks such values as unavailable when the platform or hardware does not support them.</string>

